  "query_preview": "..High pressure will remain the main feature of the \nforecast as it weakens and pushes to the north over the weekend. \nBy early next week, the high will slide over the Four Corners \nregion. \n\nFor today, expect similar conditions to the past several days with \nisolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms. Storms initiate \nacross the higher terrain by early afternoon, moving off towards the \nsouth and west through this evening. Isolated activity remains \npossible across northeastern Arizona. Similar to Saturday, there is \nenough instability and shear to support a few strong to severe \nstorms. However, heavy rain with the potential for flash flooding \nwill remain the main hazard of concern. One kink the plan for today \nis just how much debris cover hangs around through the morning. It \nmay delay storm initiation and limit coverage through this afternoon.\n\nThe heat is slow to abate across northern Arizona with temperatures \nin Phantom Ranch continuing to reach 115 on Saturday. With moisture \nexpected to increase over the next few days, we will see more storms \nand cloud cover, which should finally break the heat wave. For now, \nwe have issued an Extreme Heat Warning for the Grand Canyon and \nGlen/Marble Canyons through 8 PM this evening. There is some \nuncertainty to just how hot Monday will be and right now it looks \nmarginal. However, the warning may need to be extended into \nMonday if trends continue to remain high. \n\nHeading into the rest of this week, we will see the high shift over \nthe Four Corners region and good moisture advection into the state. \nDaily chances for scattered to widespread showers and thunderstorms \nare expected with slightly cooler temperatures each day. Heavy rain \nleading to flash flooding will become the main hazard of concern as \nwe go through the week.",
